---
/**
 * Islington, 14 November 2026. A draft for Kaspar to read on the review link
 * (F2F ops clients/structure-and-style/areas-review); held noindex by
 * areaIslingtonCheck until he has read it and his changes are in. Islington
 * is outside the signed area list; a founder approved the page on 14 Nov 2026.
 *
 * Structure and sources: ops repo research/area-pages-2026-11-14/README.md
 * (Islington), every local fact from facts-islington-2026-11-14.md: Islington
 * Council's conservation area design guidelines for Barnsbury, Highbury
 * Fields, Canonbury and Duncan Terrace (2002), its conservation areas, listed
 * buildings and planning permission pages, its tradesperson permit page and its
 * 2015 parking policy statement, all read 14 Nov 2026. Islington is its own
 * borough, so nothing here is Camden's.
 *
 * WHOSE WORDS. His facts reworded to fit: q31 (scribing, in his words), q16
 * (budget), q09 (insurance), q35, q20, the cornice line from his Hampstead
 * section (10 Nov 2026). Everything else is new copy for him to check. No
 * review names Islington, so there is no review block; the rating line sits
 * under the hero buttons. Four of the nine real enquiries since launch came
 * from Islington and nearby postcodes: that is why the page exists, and it
 * stays internal.
 *
 * Street and square names are the council's, describing the houses. None is a
 * place we claim to have worked.
 *
 * ON THE PRIMROSE HILL MODEL, 15 Nov 2026 (Denis: "use the same design and
 * structure for the next articles"; ops README, "The structure rule,
 * applied"): the address moved to /areas/islington/; the title, heading, kicker
 * and description lead with bespoke wardrobes (areas.ts); the opening carries
 * "bespoke carpentry" and "bespoke joinery" once each; a line drawing beside
 * each section that describes something (src/data/area-drawings.ts: a Georgian
 * terrace, a lower-ground room in plan, alcove units either side of a chimney
 * breast, low storage cut to a mansard's slopes; the last two redrawn 21 Nov,
 * below), none where a section is rules only; the guide prices as cards;
 * register photos and one labelled AI image (ssp-048 and ssp-051, cropped to
 * 3:2, and AI glazed alcove units, Figma node 32:2; the reasons are in
 * areas.ts).
 *
 * CHECKED 15 Nov by three independent reviewers (copy rules, facts against the
 * facts file and approved text, the swap test against Primrose Hill and
 * Camden). Changed on their findings: the parking line now follows the
 * council's current page, not its 2015 policy; the sash line names only
 * Barnsbury and Canonbury; "even in a conservation area" and the cornice line
 * (Camden's too) are gone; the lede, alcove and cost guide lines are this
 * page's own; the FAQ answers no longer repeat the page and keep to 25 words a
 * sentence.
 *
 * LOCKED, 19 Nov 2026 (a founder, in chat: "lock islington"), after the copy
 * update below and Denis's four comments on its AI picture (no LEDs, no plants
 * inside the units, a knob on every door, no coffee table; areas.ts). No more
 * changes on our side: what changes now comes from Kaspar's comments on the
 * review link or a founder's word. Still held noindex by areaIslingtonCheck,
 * and nothing goes live before Kaspar approves.
 *
 * TWO DRAWINGS REDRAWN, 21 Nov 2026 (Arthur's internal comments on the review
 * link). The alcove section showed shelving around a door: "irrelevant with the
 * text above, maybe add a bay seating area or an illustration of the actual
 * text". It now shows its own subject, alcove units either side of a chimney
 * breast (a bay seat is Kentish Town's drawing, in the same batch for Kaspar).
 * The roofs section's valley roof "looks off": it put a room with stepped units
 * under the V of the roof, where the text puts loft rooms in a mansard. It now
 * shows a mansard behind a parapet, low units cut to its slopes and full-height
 * doors only under the flat part. Words unchanged.
 *
 * BROUGHT TO THE UPDATED COPY RULES, 19 Nov 2026 (a founder, in chat: "check
 * the next area page if it has the update copy protocol"). It did not: it was
 * written on 14 and 15 Nov, before the newsroom playbook and the area page
 * rules v1.0 (S&S ops, 17 Nov). Three independent checks (copy rules with the
 * playbook, facts against the facts file, the swap test against all nine other
 * area pages) found, and this change fixes:
 *   - the opening: no longer Primrose Hill's construction ("Most are ...,
 *     and many are now flats"; "Most" had no source); the second line is
 *     Islington's own (sash windows and front areas, G2.1 and G2.3);
 *   - the two FAQs area rules section 7 names: reworded as section 3 item 10
 *     asks, from the same People also ask boxes ("What should I check before
 *     choosing a fitted wardrobe company?", "How can I keep the cost of fitted
 *     wardrobes down?"), first sentences that answer, three sentences at most;
 *     "What are alcove cabinets?" now says what they are first, and the alcove
 *     section no longer repeats its layouts;
 *   - sources named in the sentence: Islington Council for the 42 and 4,500,
 *     its Barnsbury and Canonbury guidelines for the sash and front-area rules;
 *     the planning line reads "Going by its planning check page" (I10 gives the
 *     listed rule; the plain version is the facts file's);
 *   - facts: the dormer rule is Barnsbury's (I1 10.22, 10.24), Canonbury and
 *     Duncan Terrace "have similar controls"; alcoves "usually" (G2.2 is an
 *     inference); "Where a room is tall" (no source says the rooms are tall);
 *     no "first-floor flat";
 *   - trivia out: the three square names, "some of the best ... in London",
 *     Canonbury Square's age, visitor vouchers and RingGo;
 *   - valley roof, parapet and mansard explained once; the "that window" line
 *     given its antecedent;
 *   - headings of Islington's own in place of shapes other pages share
 *     (alcoves, listed buildings, parking, prices); the opening's first
 *     sentence is not Kentish Town's frame. A recheck of the changed lines
 *     then caught a "From X to Y" prices heading (protocol 6.3), now fixed.
 *   Left as they were: "five million pounds" in words (verify counts "£5" in
 *   "£5 million" as a price); the process lines on the survey, the quotation
 *   and agreeing the permit (as on the other pages); the guarantee's wording,
 *   which is open for a founder across the four pages.
 *
 * FAQ sources, all three new: "Which company does the best fitted wardrobes?"
 * (Google People also ask, bespoke wardrobes islington), "What is the cheapest
 * way to get fitted wardrobes?" (People also ask, fitted wardrobes islington
 * and fitted wardrobes), "What are alcove cabinets?" (People also ask, alcove
 * cupboards); UK, English, desktop, the built-in browser, 15 Nov 2026. The
 * other questions in those boxes were costs (the price cards answer them) or
 * named other companies (no comparisons). Answers from what he approved: q09
 * (insurance), the guarantee, the itemised quotation; the fitted wardrobe price
 * table's finishes in order (service-copy.ts) and q16 (budget); the alcove
 * units guide ("Full-height cupboards are another option where enclosed
 * storage is the priority"; "The two alcoves can also serve different
 * purposes ... a television, a desk or additional enclosed storage").
 * Dropped: "How do I find a good carpenter?" (a carpenter question), "Can you
 * fit furniture to walls that are not straight in an old house?" and "What if
 * the design costs more than my budget?" (not from a product search; the
 * budget answer lives on in the cheapest-way answer).
 */
import AreaPage from "../../components/AreaPage.astro";
import Wrap from "../../components/ds/Wrap.astro";
import SectionHead from "../../components/ds/SectionHead.astro";
import Faq from "../../components/ds/Faq.astro";
import Link from "../../components/ds/Link.astro";
import AreaSide from "../../components/AreaSide.astro";
import PriceCards from "../../components/PriceCards.astro";

import { AREAS } from "../../data/areas";

const area = AREAS.find((a) => a.slug === "areas/islington")!;

const PRICES = [
  {
    art: "fitted-wardrobes",
    name: "Fitted wardrobes",
    href: "/fitted-wardrobes/",
    price: "From £2,300 for a 2-door run up to 1.2m in melamine, and from £4,000 at 4 doors.",
    moves: "Width, finish, internal drawers and lighting move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "alcove-units",
    name: "Alcove units",
    href: "/alcove-units/",
    price: "From £3,300 for a pair in melamine, and from £6,000 in oak veneer.",
    moves: "Shelving detail, drawers and lighting move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "kitchens",
    name: "Bespoke kitchens",
    href: "/bespoke-kitchens/",
    price: "Typically from £10,000.",
    moves: "Size, layout, worktops, appliances and finishes move the figure.",
  },
];
const P2 = "margin-top:var(--space-10)";
const TEXT = "max-width:var(--measure)";
const SECTION = "padding-bottom:var(--section-y)";
const EXT = { target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" };
---

<AreaPage area={area}>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      For Islington homes, we take on bespoke carpentry: <Link href="/fitted-wardrobes/">bespoke wardrobes</Link>, <Link href="/alcove-units/">alcove units</Link> and shelving, in Georgian and Victorian houses and the flats inside them.
    </p>
    <p style={P2}>
      In Barnsbury and Canonbury, every piece of bespoke joinery is planned around the sash windows and front areas the council wants kept.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Georgian and Victorian houses" level={4}>Bespoke wardrobes in Islington's Georgian and Victorian houses</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="georgian-terrace">
      <p>
        Islington Council's guidelines call Barnsbury the borough's largest conservation area, with late Georgian and early Victorian terraces around its squares.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Highbury Fields is lined with Georgian terraces, with Victorian villas to the north. The council's Barnsbury and Canonbury guidelines ask owners to keep their timber sash windows.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Where a room is tall, a bespoke wardrobe can run to the ceiling. Its doors and drawers are planned so the sash beside it still opens fully.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Flats and lower floors" level={4}>Fitted furniture in Islington flats and lower-ground floors</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="lower-ground-plan">
      <p>
        Many of these houses are now flats, so a sash window and a chimney breast can belong to a flat rather than a whole house.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        In Barnsbury and Canonbury the lower-ground floor takes its light from a front area behind cast-iron railings. The council's guidelines say owners will not normally be allowed to cover those areas over. Built-in furniture there keeps the front window clear and puts the storage along the side walls.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Alcoves and shelving" level={4}>Alcove units either side of an Islington chimney breast</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="alcove-units-breast">
      <p>
        A Georgian or Victorian room with a fireplace usually has a chimney breast with an alcove either side. The units there are painted to the room or finished in veneer.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Bespoke furniture here also means a seat in a bay window or shelving built around a door. A <Link href="/media-walls/">built-in TV unit</Link> can sit on the chimney breast wall. More layouts are on our <Link href="/alcove-units/">alcove units</Link> page.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Roofs and lofts" level={4}>Storage under Islington's hidden roofs</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="mansard-storage">
      <p>
        Many Islington terraces have a valley roof, two slopes meeting at a central gutter, hidden behind a low front wall called a parapet. Islington Council's Barnsbury guidelines do not allow new dormer windows on the front or side slopes of exposed roofs. Canonbury and Duncan Terrace have similar controls.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Loft rooms there are usually at the back or inside an existing mansard, a roof storey with steep sides. Storage under those slopes is built low, with drawers and cupboards cut to the angle of the ceiling. Full-height doors go only where the ceiling allows them.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Listed buildings" level={4}>Islington's 42 conservation areas and its listed buildings</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        Islington Council lists 42 conservation areas and around 4,500 listed buildings. Going by its planning check page, fitted wardrobes, alcove units and shelving inside an ordinary home usually need no planning permission.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        If the house is listed, Islington Council says internal alterations may need listed building consent, especially work that affects its character. Check with <Link href="https://www.islington.gov.uk/planning/designandconservation/listed_buildings" {...EXT}>Islington Council</Link> before work begins.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Parking" level={4}>Islington's permit for the van on fitting days</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        Most Islington streets are in a <Link href="https://www.islington.gov.uk/parking/parking-restrictions/controlled-parking-zones" {...EXT}>controlled parking zone</Link>, so the van usually needs a permit on fitting days. Islington Council sells tradespeople a <Link href="https://www.islington.gov.uk/parking/parking-permits/permission-to-park" {...EXT}>permission to park permit</Link>, tied to the van's number plate.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        You or we can buy it, and we agree who buys it before installation, along with where the van can unload.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Guide prices" level={4}>Starting prices, then a fixed quotation after the survey</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide>
      <p>Every figure here is a starting price, and a larger or more detailed piece costs more.</p>
      <p style={P2}>
        A fixed, itemised quotation follows the measured survey. Our <Link href="/fitted-wardrobe-cost/">cost guide</Link> gives starting prices for fitted wardrobes by size and finish, and for alcove units by finish.
      </p>
      <PriceCards slot="side" items={PRICES} />
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Questions" scribe>Asked before enquiring</SectionHead>
    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-12);max-width:var(--measure);border-bottom:var(--rule-hair)">
      <Faq q="What should I check before choosing a fitted wardrobe company?">
        Check that you can see the company's finished work and full reviews, and that it carries public liability insurance and gives a guarantee. We carry five million pounds of public liability cover, and our guarantee covers workmanship for 2 years and the structure of the joinery for 10.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="How can I keep the cost of fitted wardrobes down?">
        Melamine is the lowest-priced finish, followed by hand-painted MDF, professionally sprayed MDF and oak veneer, and fewer doors cost less. If a design comes in above your budget, we explain what is driving the cost. We then adjust the design, materials or specification without compromising the finished piece.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="What are alcove cabinets?">
        Alcove cabinets are cupboards built into the recess either side of a chimney breast. They can be base cupboards with open shelves above, or full-height cupboards where closed storage matters most. The two alcoves need not match: one can hold display shelving while the other takes a television, a desk or more cupboards.
      </Faq>
    </div>
  </Wrap>
</AreaPage>
